# Constitution Governance

Before writing ANY code in this project, you MUST:

1. **Read `AGENTS.md`** in the repo root. It is the project constitution (single source of truth).
2. **Follow ALL rules** defined there. They override your default behavior.
3. **Check `.cursor/rules/`** for fine-grained, glob-scoped policies.
4. **Never violate modularity.** Do not add logic to `main.py` or `App.tsx`. Extract to modules.
5. **Keep files one concern.** Past 400 lines a file is split or states why it is one concern; nothing passes 800; never squeeze a file to fit a number (AGENTS.md §2.3). Run `py -3 tools/maintainer_checks.py --gate --base origin/master` before you push.
6. **Log your work.** Record behavior changes, bug causes, and verification in the PR body -- there is no changelog; **MUST** run `py -3 tools/deferred_log.py status` **before any fix**, and open or update a GitHub Issue labeled `deferred` after parking a known bug or feature (see `deferred-log.mdc`); write the task narrative in the PR body (`.github/pull_request_template.md`), or under `knowledge/task-log/` when there is no PR (see `task-log.mdc`).
7. **PR-first delivery** for every code/config/CI/security/rule task: clean start from `origin/master`, ready (non-draft) PR as the finish line, head deleted after merge. The law is `AGENTS.md` §5.1; issue metadata and the [Nova Delivery](https://github.com/users/aaltaay/projects/1) board are `github-delivery.mdc`.
8. **Mind the always-on budget.** `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and every `alwaysApply: true` rule load on every request; `maintainer_checks.py` caps their byte total (`always_on_budget`, ADR 051). New law goes in one home; a rule that a script enforces is a pointer; mechanics and incident stories live behind `globs`, in an ADR, or on the issue.

## Rule Hierarchy (highest to lowest)

1. `AGENTS.md` -- Project Constitution (supreme law)
2. `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` -- Fine-grained enforcement policies
3. Agent system prompts -- Default AI behavior (overridden by above)

## Quick Reference: Common Violations to Avoid

- ❌ Adding a function to `backend/main.py` → put it in the correct module
- ❌ Adding a component to `frontend/src/App.tsx` → put it in `components/`
- ❌ Hardcoding a number or string → put it in `constants.py` / `constants.ts`
- ❌ Direct-pushing product work to `master` -- use a focused branch and PR
- ❌ Starting edits on a dirty worktree or a stale local `master` -- fetch and branch from `origin/master`
- ❌ Ending a coding session with local-only commits, unpushed commits, or "I'll open the PR later"
- ❌ Ending a session with a dirty tree, a `git stash`, or an abandoned worktree -- commit `wip:` on the branch, remove your worktree, `py -3 tools/repo_hygiene.py status` (workspace-hygiene.mdc)
- ❌ Parking a shippable PR as draft because CI is still running
- ❌ Leaving a shippable PR open and idle -- Actions merges ready PRs; do not wait for "please merge"
- ❌ Leaving a merged or closed PR's head branch on origin -- delete it in the same session
- ❌ Claiming an issue is on Nova Delivery after a 403 / missing `project` scope -- report the limitation; do not recreate https://github.com/users/aaltaay/projects/1
- ❌ Recreating a retired ledger (`CHANGELOG.md`, `BACKLOG.md`, `PROBLEM_LOG.md`) → the PR body is the record
- ❌ Claiming a bug fix without evidence in its PR or issue
- ❌ Parking a known bug or feature without a GitHub Issue labeled `deferred` → dump in agent-memory Backlog is not enough
- ❌ Starting a fix that is already an open `deferred` issue without reading that issue first
- ❌ Swallowing an error silently → log it, fix root cause, record the cause and fix in the PR/issue

## Context economy

- Do not re-read files already provided in this session's context.
- Prefer Grep/Glob over reading whole files longer than ~400 lines.
- Cite code references instead of pasting file contents into replies.
